---
import { storyEyebrow, storyTitle } from '../data/stories';
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Reconstruction from '../components/Reconstruction.astro';
import Chevron from '../components/Chevron.astro';
import LinkRow from '../components/LinkRow.astro';

const SITE = 'https://alucify.ai/';
const APP = 'https://alucify-screens.fly.dev/#/dashboard';
const FIGMA = 'https://www.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1';
const FIGMA_EMBED =
  'https://embed.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1&embed-host=share';

/** Where the work sits, and where it moves as context improves. */
const allocation = [
  ['Agent-ready', 'The context is strong enough for an agent to proceed.'],
  ['Needs clarification', 'A person must resolve an ambiguity first.'],
  ['Requires expert judgement', 'The possible system-wide impact needs closer review.'],
];

const flows = [
  ['First run', ['Sign in', 'Connect source', 'Build context', 'Ready']],
  ['Artifacts', ['Dashboard', 'Artifact', 'Audit', 'Re-audit']],
  ['Coverage', ['Dashboard', 'Coverage summary', 'Decision detail']],
  ['Impact', ['Dashboard', 'Impact summary', 'Concept detail']],
];

/** Engagement results from the Alucify system, not from the interface alone. */
const evidence = [
  ['15%', 'Requirements gap identified', 'Missing intent became visible before it became code.'],
  ['97%', 'Prototype fidelity', 'The implemented product preserved the design decisions.'],
  ['62.5%', 'Implementation time saved', 'Better context reduced interpretation and rework.'],
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title={storyTitle('/alucify')}
  metaTitle="Alucify — the control layer for agentic software development | Tyler Young"
  description="Coding agents can write software faster than teams can specify, review and absorb it. Alucify connects requirements, schemas, interfaces, code and tests in one queryable model, then shows where context is missing, what a change would affect, and where a person still has to decide."
  eyebrow="Semantic intelligence for software teams"
  meta={[
    ['Company', 'Semantic intelligence for existing software'],
    ['Contribution', 'Product design · information architecture · company narrative'],
    ['Scope', '4 connected flows · 20 documented screens'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s permission'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Alucify helps people and coding agents understand complex codebases. I
    designed the product surfaces that reveal missing context, predict the
    effects of a change, and show where people still need to make a decision.
  </p>

  <LinkRow
    links={[
      { text: 'Open the working screens', href: APP },
      { text: 'Open the design file', href: FIGMA },
      { text: 'Open the company site', href: SITE },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Coding agents can write software faster than teams can specify, review, and
    absorb it. But commercial software is rarely a clean project. Years of
    requirements, dependencies, and exceptions shape every codebase.
  </p>
  <p>
    That context is scattered. Product managers know the intent. Engineers know
    the architectural history. The code reflects another version of the truth.
    Without those relationships, an agent can write code that looks reasonable
    but is wrong for the system.
  </p>

  <h2>Showing what to do next</h2>
  <p>
    Alucify connects requirements, schemas, interfaces, code, and tests in a
    queryable model called the AppGraph. For each task, it gives an agent the
    relevant part of the system instead of an undifferentiated mass of context.
  </p>
  <p>
    The AppGraph can produce more evidence than a person can use at once. Raw
    graphs and scores would simply shift the interpretation work to the user. I
    designed the product around the decisions that evidence should support.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every score links to its source. Verified context is distinct from agent
    inference. Each finding explains why it matters, not just how severe it is.
    The product informs the decision without pretending to make it.
  </p>
  <p>The dashboard starts with three questions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What has been created?</li>
    <li>Which decisions are grounded in evidence?</li>
    <li>Where could a change spread?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Agent hours and token use appear alongside these signals. They estimate
    effort rather than present a bill.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="dashboard"
    label="Dashboard"
    title="Alucify code-review dashboard reconstruction"
    caption="Three starting points, with no single score presented as a verdict."
    eager
  />

  <h2>Three analyses, one purpose</h2>
  <p>
    Each analysis answers a question the team would otherwise have to guess at.
    Each gives the final decision back to the team.
  </p>

  <h3>Where does the team still need to agree?</h3>
  <p>
    Coverage Analysis shows how much of a decision rests on verified context and
    how much depends on inference.
  </p>
  <p>
    Decisions with missing context appear first because those are the places
    where an agent is most likely to invent an answer. The coverage score is not
    a grade. It shows the team where to begin a conversation.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="coverage"
    label="Coverage analysis"
    title="Alucify coverage-analysis reconstruction"
    caption="Every summary links to a named decision and its supporting evidence. The sample score is calculated from the 27 decisions below it."
  />

  <h3>What would this change affect?</h3>
  <p>
    Impact Analysis checks a proposed change against the AppGraph before
    implementation begins. It shows how far the change could spread, what might
    break, what data it could expose, and which behavior may stop working.
  </p>
  <p>
    The team can then revise an unclear requirement before an agent builds the
    wrong interpretation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every severity level includes a label, a marker, and an explanation, so the
    meaning never depends on colour alone.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="impact-detail"
    label="Impact detail"
    title="Alucify impact-detail reconstruction"
    caption="Each finding names the affected concept, explains the risk, and links back to the evidence."
  />

  <h3>Which conventions were never documented?</h3>
  <p>
    Mature codebases contain conventions that most files follow but no one wrote
    down. These rules often live in one person’s memory until that person
    leaves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Invariance Analysis finds those patterns in the code and identifies
    exceptions. For example, 48 of 49 related tests may pair setup with cleanup
    while one does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some exceptions are mistakes. Others are intentional. Alucify presents the
    evidence and leaves that judgement to the team.
  </p>
  <p>
    Together, the three analyses narrow where a person needs to look. None
    claims to make the decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the product hands the work back</h2>
  <p>
    A coverage gap means someone needs to clarify the intent. An impact finding
    means someone needs to judge the risk. Alucify sorts work into three states.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      allocation.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    These states change as the available context improves. That is the reason to
    measure them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The product is equally clear about the status of its own analysis. A result
    may still be running, return nothing, or become outdated after the
    repository changes. When an audit is stale, Alucify says so and places the
    corrective action beside the warning.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="artifact-reaudit"
    label="Stale audit"
    title="Alucify stale-audit reconstruction"
    caption="The product identifies an outdated result and shows how to refresh it."
  />

  <h2>Twenty screens across four flows</h2>
  <p>
    Onboarding establishes the codebase context used by every later analysis.
    The dashboard provides orientation. The detailed views let users trace each
    signal back to its evidence.
  </p>

  <div class="flows">
    <p class="sr-only">
      Four flows. First run goes from sign in, to connect source, to build
      context, to ready. The other three each begin at the dashboard: artifacts
      continues to artifact, then audit, then re-audit; coverage continues to the
      coverage summary and then a decision detail; impact continues to the impact
      summary and then a concept detail.
    </p>
    {
      flows.map(([name, steps]) => (
        <div class="flow" aria-hidden="true">
          <p class="flow-name">{name}</p>
          <ol class="flow-steps">
            {steps.map((step) => (
              <li class:list={['flow-step', { hub: step === 'Dashboard' }]}>{step}</li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <figure class="recon figma">
    <div class="recon-head">
      <span class="recon-label">Complete design file</span>
      <span class="recon-host">20 screens · 4 flows</span>
      <a class="recon-open" href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open in Figma<Chevron weight="bold" />
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="recon-viewport figma-viewport">
      <p class="recon-fallback">
        Complete design file — <a href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open in Figma</a>
      </p>
      <iframe
        src={FIGMA_EMBED}
        title="The Alucify design file — twenty screens across four flows"
        loading="lazy"
        referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
        allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p>
    During the LangBuilder engagement, Alucify found a requirements gap before
    implementation. Prototypes grounded in the AppGraph also remained close to
    the finished product, and the team reported less implementation time.
  </p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      evidence.map(([figure, label, why]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-figure">{figure}</span>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{why}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    These figures describe the Alucify system and the engagement as a whole.
    They are not results of the interface design alone.
  </p>

  <h2>What I did</h2>
  <p>I was the product designer.</p>
  <p>
    Alucify sits between complex machine analysis and consequential human
    decisions. My job was to make that boundary clear: what the system knows,
    what it inferred, what a change could affect, and where a person needs to
    step in.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also designed the company website and its narrative. Alucify spans code
    intelligence, development infrastructure, and agent governance. Because that
    category has no familiar name, the story begins with a problem software
    teams already recognize, then introduces the model behind the solution.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result makes three things easier to see: the codebase, the uncertainty
    within it, and the consequences of a change.
  </p>

  <h2>Notes</h2>
  <div class="disclosure">
    <p>
      The interactive screens are a working reconstruction of my designs. They
      allow people to explore the product states, but they are not the shipping
      product.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tenant, people, and measurements shown in the reconstruction are
      fictional. The sample summaries match their underlying rows but do not
      represent customer results.
    </p>
    <p>The LangBuilder figures are company-level engagement results.</p>
    <p>
      The public company website still uses Alucify’s earlier positioning. This
      case study describes its current narrative.
    </p>
    <p>This work appears with the founder’s permission.</p>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>
